Vim operates on a grammar: operators (d, c, y) take motions or text objects as
their "noun." Once that clicks, you stop memorizing commands and start
composing them.
%
The dot command (.) repeats your last change. Design your edits to be
repeatable and a single keystroke does the work of many.
%
ci" changes the contents of a double-quoted string — cursor anywhere inside it.
ci( does the same for parentheses. You don't need to move to the delimiter first.
%
Text objects respect structure. diw deletes a word. daw deletes the word and
its surrounding whitespace. The difference matters more than you'd expect.
%
% jumps between matching brackets, braces, and parentheses. On a lonely
bracket with no match, it'll just sit there — much like the bracket itself.
%
You have 26 lowercase marks per file and 26 uppercase marks across all files.
Set one with m{letter}. Jump to the exact position with `{letter}, or to the
start of the line with '{letter}.
%
Uppercase marks (A–Z) persist across sessions. mV on your vimrc, then 'V to
open it from anywhere, any time. No more :e ~/.vimrc.
%
Vim has over 30 registers. " is the unnamed register (last yank or delete).
0 is the yank register — it holds your last yank and is not overwritten by
deletes. Use "0p when you want what you yanked, not what you deleted.
%
* and + are the clipboard registers. "+y yanks to the system clipboard.
"+p pastes from it. Your mouse doesn't have to touch the keyboard.
%
/ holds your last search pattern. : holds your last command. You can paste
them into the buffer with Ctrl-R / and Ctrl-R : in insert mode.
%
Ctrl-R {register} in insert mode pastes any register without leaving insert
mode. Ctrl-R " pastes the unnamed register. Ctrl-R 0 pastes your last yank.
%
Record a macro with q{letter}, make your changes, stop with q. Replay with
@{letter}. Replay the last macro again with @@. Give it a count: 50@@ to run
it fifty times.
%
To edit a macro, :put {letter} to dump its contents into the buffer, edit
the text, then yank it back with "{letter}y$. Macros are just text.
%
Make your macros position-independent. Move to a landmark (word boundary,
specific character) rather than pressing l seven times. A macro that depends
on a fixed offset is a macro waiting to break.
%
:norm {command} runs a normal-mode command on every line in a range.
:%norm A; appends a semicolon to every line in the file.
%
The :global command (:g) is a filter that runs an Ex command on every line
matching a pattern. :g/TODO/d deletes every line containing "TODO."
:g/pattern/p prints every matching line — a poor man's grep, already open in
your buffer.
%
:g/^$/d deletes all blank lines. :g/./,/^$/-j joins each block of consecutive
non-empty lines. :g is surprisingly powerful once you stop being afraid of it.
%
To search and replace interactively, use :%s/old/new/gc. The c flag prompts
you for each match: y to replace, n to skip, a to replace all remaining.
%
\v in a search pattern enables "very magic" mode, where most characters have
special meaning without backslashes. :%s/\v(foo)(bar)/\2\1/g swaps them.
%
gn is a text object for the current search match. cgn changes it, then . will
find the next match and change it too. More surgical than a global replace.
%
n and N navigate search matches. But * searches for the exact word under your
cursor without typing anything. # searches backward. Combine with cgn for
precision find-and-replace.
%
Ctrl-O and Ctrl-I move backward and forward through your jump list —
every position Vim considered a "jump." You can retrace your steps across
files, not just lines.
%
'' (two single quotes) jumps to where you were before your last jump. A
fast way to toggle between two distant positions in a file.
%
gi jumps back to where you last left insert mode and re-enters it.
Useful when you interrupted an edit to check something elsewhere.
%
g; and g, walk backward and forward through your change list — every
position where you made an edit. A chronological breadcrumb trail.
%
zt, zz, and zb scroll the viewport so the current line sits at the top,
center, or bottom of the screen, without moving the cursor.
%
H, M, and L move the cursor to the top, middle, or bottom of the visible
screen. No scrolling needed — just reposition and keep editing.
%
Ctrl-D and Ctrl-U scroll half a page down or up. Ctrl-F and Ctrl-B scroll a
full page. Use them instead of holding j or k like a barbarian.
%
f{char} jumps the cursor forward to the next occurrence of a character on the
current line. ; repeats it. , reverses it. This is faster than you think.
%
t{char} jumps to just before a character. dt, deletes everything up to the
next comma. ct) changes everything up to the closing parenthesis. The t is
for "till."
%
W, B, and E operate on WORDS — whitespace-delimited tokens — rather than
vim-defined words. Useful for jumping over dotted identifiers and
punctuation-heavy code.
%
( and ) jump by sentences. { and } jump by paragraphs. For code, paragraphs
are often contiguous non-blank-line blocks, which maps surprisingly well to
function boundaries.
%
=i{ re-indents the entire block under the cursor. Place your cursor anywhere
inside braces and let Vim fix the indentation. Works for any bracket pair.
%
gq{motion} reflows text to the value of 'textwidth'. gqip reformats the
current paragraph. Useful for keeping prose and comments from sprawling.
%
gU{motion} uppercases, gu{motion} lowercases, g~{motion} swaps case.
gUiw uppercases the current word without selecting it first.
%
Ctrl-A increments the number under the cursor. Ctrl-X decrements it. Give
either a count: 10 Ctrl-A adds ten. Works on hex values too, if 'nrformats'
includes hex.
%
Visual block mode (Ctrl-V) lets you select a rectangular column of text.
I in visual block mode inserts text at the start of every selected line
simultaneously. Essential for adding or removing aligned prefixes.
%
>> and << indent and dedent the current line. In visual mode, they work on
the selection and keep you in visual mode so you can indent repeatedly.
%
:sort sorts lines alphabetically. :sort! reverses it. :sort n sorts
numerically. Select a range in visual mode first to sort only part of a file.
%
J joins the current line with the line below, inserting a space between them.
gJ does the same without the space. 5J joins five lines.
%
o opens a new line below and enters insert mode. O opens one above.
These are almost always faster than End, Enter, or Home, i.
%
A appends at end of line. I inserts at the first non-blank character.
You rarely need to navigate to either end manually.
%
S (uppercase) deletes the current line's contents, respects indentation, and
enters insert mode. For rewriting a line from the correct indent level.
%
R enters replace mode, overwriting characters as you type until Esc. A scalpel
for when you need to overwrite a fixed-width field without disturbing what
follows.
%
q: opens the command-line history in an editable buffer. q/ does the same for
search history. Edit a past command like any text, then Enter to run it.
%
Ctrl-R = in insert mode opens an expression register. Type any Vimscript
expression, press Enter, and the result is inserted into the buffer.
Evaluate math, call functions, or read variables — all without leaving insert.
%
:read !{command} inserts the output of a shell command below the cursor.
:read !date stamps the current time into your document. Pipes flow both ways.
%
:{range}!{command} filters the selected lines through an external command and
replaces them with its output. :%!sort -u deduplicates and sorts the entire
file using your shell's sort. Vim and Unix compose.
%
:split and :vsplit open horizontal and vertical splits. Ctrl-W followed by
hjkl navigates between them. Ctrl-W = equalizes their sizes.
%
Ctrl-W T moves the current split into its own tab. Ctrl-W o closes all other
splits, leaving only the current window. Handy when a split has outlived its
usefulness.
%
Buffers are not tabs. A buffer is an open file. A window is a viewport into a
buffer. A tab is a layout of windows. You can view the same buffer in multiple
windows simultaneously.
%
:ls lists all open buffers. :b {partial name} jumps to a buffer by name —
Tab-completion works. :bd closes one. You don't need a plugin to manage
a modest buffer list.
%
:args {glob} loads a set of files into the argument list. :argdo {command}
runs a command on every file in it. Bulk refactoring across a directory
without leaving Vim.
%
:vimgrep /{pattern}/g ** populates the quickfix list with every match across
your project. :copen shows the list; Enter jumps to each hit. A built-in
project-wide search.
%
:cdo {command} runs a command on every entry in the quickfix list.
After a :vimgrep, :cdo s/old/new/ | update renames across every matching file.
%
:set spell enables spell-checking. ]s jumps to the next misspelling. z= over a
word shows suggestions. zg adds a word to your dictionary. zw marks one as
wrong.
%
:set colorcolumn=80 draws a vertical guide at column 80. A gentle reminder
rather than a hard wall. Pair with 'textwidth' for automatic line breaking.
%
'relativenumber' shows line distances rather than absolute numbers, turning
every jump count into a trivially readable value. 14j. 6k. No arithmetic.
%
Folding with 'foldmethod=indent' or 'foldmethod=syntax' collapses structure
automatically. za toggles a fold. zM closes all folds. zR opens all folds.
%
:mksession saves the entire editor state — windows, buffers, folds, cursor
positions — to a file. vim -S Session.vim restores it exactly. Leave. Come
back. Continue.
%
autocmd BufWritePre * :%s/\s\+$//e automatically strips trailing whitespace on
every save. A small ceremony that keeps diffs clean.
%
iabbrev is Vim's autocorrect for insert mode. iabbrev teh the fixes a
perennial typo silently and permanently. You can also use it to expand
abbreviations into boilerplate.
%
:help {topic} is the fastest documentation in any editor. :help 'option'
looks up a setting. :help :command looks up a command. :help function()
looks up a function. The entire manual is one keystroke away.
%
:helpgrep {pattern} searches the full Vim documentation for a pattern and
populates the quickfix list. If you don't know the right :help keyword,
this finds it.
%
Don't add a mapping until you've used the bare command long enough to feel the
friction. Map the friction, not the imagined convenience.
%
Use nnoremap, inoremap, vnoremap — not nmap, imap, vmap. The noremap variants
don't chain through other mappings. The difference surfaces as a subtle,
maddening bug if you ever get it wrong.
%
Write your vimrc incrementally. Add one setting, learn it, keep it if it
helps. A vimrc copied wholesale from a stranger is a collection of answers
to questions you haven't asked yet.
%
:set paste and :set nopaste toggle paste mode, which disables autoindent and
remapping during a paste. If your terminal pastes look like a staircase, this
is why. (Or just use Ctrl-Shift-V, or "+p.)
%
Digraphs let you insert characters not on your keyboard. In insert mode,
Ctrl-K followed by two characters produces the digraph. Ctrl-K a: produces ä.
:digraphs lists them all.
%
Ctrl-N and Ctrl-P in insert mode trigger keyword completion from the current
buffer and all open buffers. No plugin required for basic autocomplete.
%
Ctrl-X Ctrl-F completes file paths. Ctrl-X Ctrl-L completes whole lines.
Ctrl-X Ctrl-] completes from ctags. Each Ctrl-X sub-mode is a specialized
completion source.
%
:earlier and :later travel through undo history by time rather than edit count.
:earlier 5m takes the buffer back to how it looked five minutes ago.
%
The undo tree, not the undo list. Vim's undo history is a tree: if you undo
several steps and then make a new edit, the old branch is preserved, not
erased. :undolist shows the branches. The undotree plugin makes it visual.
%
vimtutor is an interactive tutorial built into Vim. If someone you know is
learning, sit them down with vimtutor before any YouTube video. Thirty minutes
of hands-on beats two hours of watching.
%
The best Vim tip: stay in normal mode most of the time. Insert mode is for
typing. Normal mode is for thinking. The cursor resting in normal mode is not
idle — it's ready.
%
